    "text_delta": "’ or teachers’ union, contact their legal/employment representative immediately.\n   * Unions routinely handle appointment disputes. They can review the original job description, person specification, and scoring matrix to assess whether the panel deviated from the advertised criteria or breached equal opportunity regulations.\n\n3. **Examine the Criteria vs. Scoring Matrix**\n   * Once you receive the documentation, review how qualifications and experience were weighted against interview impressions. Did the panel evaluate candidates against the published job description, or were the criteria shifted after the interviews?\n\n4. **Document Everything**\n   * Note down details from the two interviews while they are fresh: the questions asked, panel reactions, any comments made about leadership style, availability, family, or \"fit,\" and the exact timeline of communications.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nAn all-male panel alone does not constitute legal proof of discrimination, but in combination with the credential contrast and delayed feedback, it is a **valid red flag**. Use this awareness to insist on full transparency and written evaluation from the school authority, ideally with guidance from your professional association.\n",
